# 7 CFD concepts the solver is built on {#ch:cfd}

This chapter collects the general ideas that every later chapter uses. Each
one is introduced on the simplest equation that shows it, and each ends with
the place where Basilisk uses it.

## Conservation laws and the finite-volume method

Most equations of fluid mechanics state that some quantity $q$ (mass,
momentum, a volume fraction) changes in a fixed region $V$ only because of
what flows through its boundary $\partial V$ and what is produced inside:
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\int_V q\,dV = -\oint_{\partial V} \mathbf{F}(q)\cdot\mathbf{n}\,dS
+ \int_V s\,dV,
\tag{7.1}
$$
where $\mathbf{F}$ is the flux of $q$ (for advection, $\mathbf{F} = q\mathbf{u}$),
$\mathbf{n}$ is the outward unit normal and $s$ a source. Applying the divergence
theorem and letting $V$ shrink to a point gives the differential form
$\partial_t q + \nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = s$.

A finite-volume method applies (7.1) to each grid cell.
For a square cell of side $\Delta$ in 2D, define the cell average
$\bar q_{ij} = \Delta^{-2}\int_{\text{cell}} q\,dV$ and the face-averaged flux,
for example $F_{{i+\frac 12},j}$ through the right face. Then
(7.1) is exact in the form
$$
\frac{d\bar q_{ij}}{dt} = \frac{F_{{i-\frac 12},j} - F_{{i+\frac 12},j}}{\Delta}
 + \frac{G_{i,j-\frac12} - G_{i,j+\frac12}}{\Delta} + \bar s_{ij},
\tag{7.2}
$$
with $F$ and $G$ the $x$ and $y$ fluxes (figure 7.1). The only
approximation is in how the face fluxes are computed from the cell averages.
Because the flux leaving cell $i$ through face ${i+\frac 12}$ is the same number that
enters cell $i+1$, the sum of $\bar q\Delta^2$ over all cells changes only by
what crosses the domain boundary. This is *discrete conservation*, and
it holds whatever formula is used for the face flux.

