it changes by the difference of the fluxes through its four faces. The flux
through a shared face is computed once and used with opposite signs by the
two cells, which makes the scheme conservative.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_fvcell.png)

> **In the code: `bcg.h`, function `advection()`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> f[] += dt*(flux.x[] - flux.x[1])/(Delta*cm[]);
> ~~~
>
> This is (7.2) integrated over one step with forward Euler.
> `flux.x[]` is the flux through the left face and `flux.x[1]`
> through the right face; `foreach_dimension()` repeats the line for $y$
> and $z$. `cm[]` is the fraction of the cell open to fluid (1 without a
> solid).

## Truncation error, order, stability and convergence

Replace derivatives by differences and the result differs from the exact
equation by a *truncation error*. For example, by Taylor expansion,
$$
\frac{q_{i+1} - q_{i-1}}{2\Delta} = \partial_x q + \frac{\Delta^2}{6}\partial_x^3 q
+ O(\Delta^4),
$$
so the central difference has truncation error $O(\Delta^2)$ and is
*second-order accurate*: halving $\Delta$ divides the error by four. A
scheme is *consistent* if its truncation error goes to zero as $\Delta$
and $\Delta t$ go to zero.

Consistency is not enough. A scheme is *stable* if errors (round-off,
initial errors) do not grow without bound as the steps proceed. The Lax
equivalence theorem says that for a well-posed linear problem, a consistent
scheme converges to the exact solution if and only if it is stable. In
practice, every time-step restriction in Basilisk exists to keep a scheme
stable.

## Advection, upwinding and numerical diffusion {#sec:upwind}

Take the model problem $\partial_t q + c\,\partial_x q = 0$ with $c > 0$. Its
exact solution moves the initial profile to the right at speed $c$ without
changing its shape. Define the Courant number
$$
C = \frac{c\,\Delta t}{\Delta}.
$$

**First-order upwind.** Information comes from the left, so take the
face value from the upwind cell, $F_{i+\frac 12}= c\,q_i$. Then
$$
q_i^{n+1} = q_i^n - C\,(q_i^n - q_{i-1}^n).
\tag{7.4}
$$
Expanding $q_{i-1}$ and $q_i^{n+1}$ in Taylor series around $(x_i, t^n)$ and
using the equation itself to swap time derivatives for space derivatives
shows that (7.4) solves, to second order, the *modified
equation*
$$
\partial_t q + c\,\partial_x q = \underbrace{\frac{c\Delta}{2}(1 - C)}_{\nu_{\text{num}}}\,\partial_x^2 q.
\tag{7.5}
$$
Upwinding adds a diffusion $\nu_{\text{num}}$ that is not in the physics. It
vanishes only at $C = 1$. This is *numerical diffusion*, and it is why
first-order upwind smears fronts (figure 7.2).

**Second order and Godunov's theorem.** Adding a linear slope inside
the upwind cell, $F_{i+\frac 12}= c\,[q_i + \tfrac{1}{2}(1 - C)(q_{i+1} - q_i)]$
(the Lax--Wendroff flux), removes the $\partial_x^2$ error term and makes the
scheme second order. The leading error becomes a third derivative, which is
*dispersive*: different wavelengths travel at different speeds and
oscillations appear next to steep fronts. Godunov's theorem states that no
linear scheme can be both higher than first order and free of new
oscillations. The way around it is to make the scheme nonlinear: compute the
slope, then *limit* it near extrema and fronts. Such schemes are called
total-variation diminishing (TVD). The minmod limiter takes the smaller of
the left and right differences when they have the same sign and zero when
they do not.

