## The CFL condition {#sec:cfl}

In an explicit scheme the new value in cell $i$ depends on cells $i-1$, $i$
and $i+1$ at the old time. The exact solution at $(x_i, t^{n+1})$ depends on
the initial data at $x_i - c\Delta t$. If that point is outside the cells the
scheme looks at, the scheme cannot be right however small $\Delta$ is
(figure 7.3). This gives the Courant--Friedrichs--Lewy condition
$C = |c|\Delta t/\Delta\le 1$ for the stencil of (7.4). Stability
analysis gives the same bound for upwind.

![Figure 7.3: Domain of dependence. The dashed lines are characteristics
$x - ct = \text{const}$ through the new point. When $C < 1$ the
characteristic lands inside the cells used by the scheme; when $C > 1$ it
lands outside and the scheme is missing the information it needs.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_cfl.png)

Basilisk uses $C \le 0.8$ for the velocity (`CFL = 0.8` in
`centered.h`) and lowers it to $0.5$ when VOF is used
(`vof.h`); [section 17.5](17_vof#sec:vofcfl) explains why VOF needs the smaller value.

## Explicit and implicit time stepping {#sec:implicit}

For diffusion, $\partial_t q = \nu\,\partial_x^2 q$, the explicit update
$q_i^{n+1} = q_i^n + \frac{\nu\Delta t}{\Delta^2}(q_{i+1} - 2q_i + q_{i-1})^n$ is
stable only if $\nu\Delta t/\Delta^2 \le 1/2$ in 1D (and $\le 1/(2d)$ in $d$
dimensions). The limit scales with $\Delta^2$, so halving the cell size cuts the
allowed step by four. The implicit (backward Euler) update
$$
q_i^{n+1} - \frac{\nu\Delta t}{\Delta^2}(q_{i+1} - 2q_i + q_{i-1})^{n+1} = q_i^n
\tag{7.6}
$$
is stable for any $\Delta t$, at the price of solving a linear system every step.
Basilisk treats viscosity this way ([chapter 13](13_viscosity)) and solves the
system with the same multigrid method used for pressure.

## Incompressibility and the role of pressure {#sec:pressure-role}

For an incompressible fluid, mass conservation reduces to
$\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0$. This is a constraint, not an evolution equation: nothing in
it says how $\mathbf{u}$ changes in time. Pressure is the field that enforces it.
For constant density, taking the divergence of the momentum equation and
using $\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0$ gives
$$
\nabla^2 p = -\rho\,\nabla\cdot(\mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla\mathbf{u}),
$$
an elliptic equation: the pressure at one point depends instantly on the
velocity everywhere. Pressure acts as a Lagrange multiplier that removes
any part of the velocity change that would create or destroy volume.

**Helmholtz--Hodge decomposition.** Any smooth vector field
$\mathbf{w}$ in a domain can be split uniquely into a divergence-free part
tangent to the boundary and a gradient:
$$
\mathbf{w} = \mathbf{u}+ \nabla\phi,\qquad \nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}= 0,\quad \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{n}|_{\partial\Omega} = 0.
$$
Taking the divergence gives $\nabla^2\phi = \nabla\cdot\mathbf{w}$ with
$\partial_n\phi = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{n}$ on the boundary. Solving this Poisson
problem and subtracting $\nabla\phi$ *projects* $\mathbf{w}$ onto the
divergence-free fields. This is the idea of Chorin's projection method
\[[4](references)\]: advance the velocity ignoring the constraint, then project.
The scalar $\phi$ turns out to be $\Delta t\,p/\rho$. [Chapter 15](15_projection) derives
the discrete version.

## Where the unknowns live: collocated and staggered grids {#sec:checker}

If $p$ and $\mathbf{u}$ are both stored at cell centres and the pressure gradient is
computed with central differences, $\partial_x p|_i \approx (p_{i+1} -
p_{i-1})/(2\Delta)$ never uses $p_i$. A pressure field that alternates
$+1, -1, +1, \dots$ from cell to cell (a checkerboard) then has zero computed
gradient and is invisible to the momentum equation, so nothing removes it
(figure 7.4). Staggered (MAC) grids avoid this by storing each
velocity component on the faces, where $(p_{i+1} - p_i)/\Delta$ is the natural
gradient.

Basilisk uses both: the cell-centred velocity $\mathbf{u}$ (convenient for
advection, viscosity, refinement and output) and a face-normal velocity
$\mathbf{u}_f$ (the one used for fluxes, made exactly divergence-free by the
projection, with a compact face pressure gradient). The coupling between the
two is the *approximate projection* of [section 15.5](15_projection#sec:approx-proj).

![Figure 7.4: A checkerboard pressure. The central difference across the outlined
cell uses its two neighbours, which hold the same value, so the computed
gradient is zero everywhere although the pressure is far from uniform.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_checker.png)

## Representing an interface

Two-phase flows need to know where each fluid is. Basilisk's main method is
the volume-of-fluid (VOF) method: each cell stores the fraction
$f\in[0,1]$ of its volume filled with fluid 1 (water). The interface is
reconstructed inside each cut cell as a straight line (2D) or plane (3D),
and advected geometrically so that $f$ stays sharp and its total is
conserved ([chapter 17](17_vof)). Both fluids are solved as one fluid with
properties that jump across the interface ([chapter 8](08_equations)).

## Dimensionless numbers {#sec:dimless}

Before setting up a case, estimate the dimensionless numbers. They tell you
which terms matter, which modules you need and how fine the grid has to be.
With a velocity scale $U$, a length $L$, densities $\rho_1$ (heavier) and
$\rho_2$, viscosity $\mu$, surface tension $\sigma$ and gravity $g$:

| Number | Definition | What it decides |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Reynolds | $Re = \rho UL/\mu$ | inertia vs viscosity; boundary layer thickness $\sim L/\sqrt{Re}$ |
| Froude | $Fr = U/\sqrt{gL}$ | inertia vs gravity; free-surface waves |
| Weber | $We = \rho U^2L/\sigma$ | inertia vs surface tension; breakup |
| Bond | $Bo = (\rho_1 - \rho_2)gL^2/\sigma$ | gravity vs surface tension; drop and bubble shape |
| Density ratio | $\rho_1/\rho_2$ | need for momentum-conserving transport ([chapter 12](12_momentum)) |
| Courant | $C = U\Delta t/\Delta$ | time step ([section 7.4](#sec:cfl)) |
| Cells per feature | $L/\Delta$ | resolution: aim for 10--30 cells across the smallest feature |

: Table 7.1: Dimensionless numbers to estimate before a run.

Two examples of how this guides choices. Water and air have
$\rho_1/\rho_2 \approx 830$, so a free-surface flow with breaking or
splashing should use `navier-stokes/conserving.h`. For a water wave of
wavelength 1 m, $Bo \approx 10^5$ and surface tension can be left out;
for a bubble of radius 1 mm, $Bo \approx 0.1$ and surface tension
controls its shape.

